The  interface  is  targeted for route‐based VPNs.  It can tunnel
IPv4 and IPv6 traffic over either IPv4 or IPv6 and secure it with
ESP.  interfaces are dynamically created and destroyed  with  the
and  subcommands.   The  administrator must configure tunnel end‐
point addresses.  These addresses will be used for the  outer  IP
header  of  ESP  packets.   The administrator also configures the
protocol and addresses for the inner IP header with the  or  sub‐
commands,  and  modify  the  routing  table  to route the packets
through the interface.  The packet processing is similar to  over
transport mode, however the security policy management is differ‐
ent.   over  transport  mode  expects userland programs to manage
their security policies.  In contrast, manages its security poli‐
cies by itself: when the administrator sets up an  tunnel  source
and  destination  address pair, the related security policies are
created automatically in  the  kernel.   They  are  automatically
deleted when the tunnel is destroyed.  It also means that ensures
that  both  the  in and out security policy pairs exist, that is,
avoids the trouble caused when only one of the in and  out  secu‐
rity policy pair exists.  There are four security policies gener‐
ated  by one in and out pair for IPv4 and IPv6 each.  These secu‐
rity policies are equivalent to the following configuration where
src and dst are IP addresses  specified  to  the  tunnel:  spdadd
"src" "dst" ipv4 ‐P out ipsec esp/transport//unique; spdadd "dst"
"src"  ipv4 ‐P in ipsec esp/transport//unique; spdadd "src" "dst"
ipv6 ‐P out ipsec esp/transport//unique; spdadd "dst" "src"  ipv6
‐P in ipsec esp/transport//unique; The configuration will fail if
such  security  policies  already exist, and vice versa.  The re‐
lated security associates can be established  by  an  IKE  daemon
such  as They can also be manipulated manually by with the option
which sets a security policy’s unique id.  Some parameters change
the behaviour of link0 can enable NAT‐Traversal, link1 can enable
ECN friendly mode like and link2 can enable forwarding inner IPv6
packets.  Only link2 is set by default.  If  you  use  only  IPv4
packets  as  inner  packets, you would want to do ifconfig ipsec0
‐link2 to reduce security associates for IPv6 packets.   Configu‐
ration example: Out IP addr = 172.16.100.1            Out IP addr
